0	0	0	Finally, a tiny note on cynicism. When telling these stories, sometimes I end up in a pattern with the listener that annoys the shit out of me. At every beat, every moment of tension or risk, they respond with shock. Worse, when there are consequences, a day without food, an awkward interaction, a broken bike, an injury, they respond with advice. In the worst case, with a smug sense of superiority. \n"Well, you should have stayed in a hotel. It would've been much more comfortable. You can't trust strangers like that."\n"Well, you were obviously going to get lost. You should have a map with you next time."\n"Well, you should have hired a guide. Of course you got scammed without a local to help you."\nThey're unfortunately making an important but subtle mistake. Not a difference of opinion or a valid alternative perspective. A logical error. It's so easy to react this way in retrospect because they're completely ignoring the benefits. For every bad day, there are five good days. For every scam, there are ten acts of kindness and decency. The cynic is generally a wounded idealist, who once believed in a perfect world where all pleasure is given and all pain can be avoided. You just need to be savvy enough. What they share with idealists is the belief that perfect control is out there. That's obviously false. \n\nOf course we have control, but not as much as we'd hope. Each interaction is a risk. It could go well. It could go badly. Most likely, somewhere in the middle. And here you are, one tiny fragile human, buffeted by the currents of life, unsure of what will happen next. But there's a sacrifice that these extremist, idealist cynics don't want to accept. The only way to be sure of what's coming next is to retreat into predictability. To lock yourself in a dark room somewhere and never leave - something the western world is slowly enabling its citizens to do - but we'll leave the politics for another time.\n\nBut, we don't have full control. For the good, you need to risk the bad. What you get in both cases is more life. The opposite of predictable darkness: vibrant, chaotic life. For the night, sharing dinner with a family and basking in their kindness, you risk the uncomfortable night, awkwardly rejecting requests for money, visas and other favours. For the car ride to a good meal, you risk awkward advances from a man twenty-five years your senior. This time, I got both. \n\n
